  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\nIssued at 1122 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nRecord-setting heat will continue to impact the Mid-South through\nmidweek underneath a strong subtropical ridge. Conditions over\nthe next several days will mirror early summer as increased\nmoisture produces heat indices in the upper 90s to lower 100s.\nThere is potential for showers and few rumbles of thunder on\nWednesday as a cold front stalls northwest of the forecast area.\nNortheast Arkansas and the Missouri Bootheel have the greatest\npotential for rainfall at this time, but amounts will generally\nbe less than half an inch.\n\nThere is light at the end of the tunnel. Guidance continues to\nshow a shortwave trough developing over southern Manitoba on\nThursday, trekking southeast towards the Great Lakes Region. This\nsystem will eject a strong cold front into the Mid-South on\nFriday. Elevated fire weather concerns will manifest at this time\nas 20ft winds approach 20 mph in northeast Arkansas and the\nMissouri Bootheel. This, combined with dry fuels and decreasing\ndewpoints, may necessitate the issuance of a Rangeland Fire\nDanger Statement on Friday. A Red Flag Warning is not anticipated\nat this time due to forecast relative humidity remaining above\n25 percent.\n\nThe upcoming weekend features fall-like conditions with highs in\nthe 60s and 70s. Long range guidance is starting to hint at a\nshortwave ejecting out of the Rockies late Sunday into Monday.\nThis may increase our rain chances early next week, but given the\ndry air in place ahead of this system we are not anticipating a\nwidespread rainfall event.",
